// ============================================================================
// Resolution · VIRTUAL DISPATCH  (Class Hierarchy Analysis)
//
// Interface / abstract dispatch. A call on an interface- or supertype-typed
// receiver (the classic dependency-injection shape: a field/param typed to an
// interface, the concrete implementation supplied elsewhere) must reach the
// concrete override(s), not just the abstract declaration where no body lives.
//
// CHA is a sound over-approximation: the call fans out to EVERY concrete override
// in a descendant type. No reverse-hierarchy table is needed — type_ancestor is
// queried with the *ancestor* bound and the subtype free, so it enumerates all
// implementors. Narrowing to the single injected implementation is a data-flow /
// points-to concern for a later engine, not this layer.
//
// Depends only on java_method / java_type (raw EDB) plus type_ancestor and
// method_name_in_type (derived IDB) — crucially, NO expr_type dependency, so this is
// buildable before the call-resolution layer. Matching is name + parameterCount (not
// full signature): type-parameter unification is beyond Datalog.
//
// Design note: method_override leads each clause with the restrictive constraint
// (abstract method / interface type) so base is pinned to the few dispatch-relevant
// declarations. virtual_override is intentionally BROADER — it also covers a concrete
// class method overridden in a subclass (non-abstract base), which method_override
// excludes, so the CHA call graph stays sound for plain class overrides too. This
// form joins java_method on (name, parameterCount); on a ~45k-method client it added
// ~2s (measured — not the bottleneck). If a much larger codebase with very popular
// method names ever makes this join dominate, restrict it (interface/abstract only,
// i.e. the inverse of method_override) and recover concrete-class overrides
// demand-driven from resolved call sites instead.
//
// java_method (22): 0 name  7 typeRegistryLinkHash(owner)  16 methodKind
//   17 parameterCount  21 methodRegistryUniqueHash
// java_type (14): 3 typeCategory  13 hash
// ============================================================================

// ── method_override(ConcreteHash, BaseHash) ─────────────────────────────────
// A concrete instance method overrides an abstract/interface declaration of the
// same name and arity in a descendant type. Used to suppress the empty abstract
// base as a call/flow target once a concrete override exists. Each clause leads
// with the restrictive constraint (abstract method / interface type) so base is
// pinned to the few dispatch-relevant declarations before the descendant match.

// Clause 1: base method is declared abstract (abstract class or interface).
method_override(concrete, base) :- java_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, baseType, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, "ABSTRACT_METHOD", pc, _, _, _, base),
    name != "",
    type_ancestor(subType, baseType),
    subType != baseType,
    java_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, subType, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, "INSTANCE_METHOD", pc, _, _, _, concrete),
    concrete != base.

// Clause 2: base method lives in an interface type (some interface methods are
// not tagged ABSTRACT_METHOD; the type-level INTERFACE_TYPE check is the anchor).
method_override(concrete, base) :- java_type(_, _, _, "INTERFACE_TYPE", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, ifaceType),
    java_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, ifaceType, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, pc, _, _, _, base),
    name != "",
    type_ancestor(implType, ifaceType),
    java_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, implType, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, "INSTANCE_METHOD", pc, _, _, _, concrete),
    concrete != base.

// Clause 3: enum-constant body override. Enum constants with bodies
// (ADD { int apply(){…} }) are anonymous overrides of the enum's abstract method,
// but the extractor attributes BOTH the abstract declaration and each constant body
// to the enum type itself (as ABSTRACT_METHOD / ENUM_CONSTANT_METHOD) — there are no
// distinct Op$ADD subtypes and no type_ancestor edge for CHA to follow. Match the two
// method kinds within the same enum instead. (The concrete bodies are already
// reachable via method_name_in_type on an enum-typed receiver, since they are
// declared in the enum; this only suppresses the empty abstract as a flow target.)
method_override(concrete, base) :- java_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, enum, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, "ABSTRACT_METHOD", pc, _, _, _, base),
    name != "",
    java_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, enum, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, "ENUM_CONSTANT_METHOD", pc, _, _, _, concrete).

// ── virtual_override(BaseHash, OverrideHash) ────────────────────────────────
// The CHA fan-out: for a base method, every same-name/same-arity method that a
// descendant type would dispatch to. Binding baseType and leaving sub free makes
// type_ancestor enumerate all subtypes/implementors. The override is taken from
// method_name_in_type in the descendant, which includes methods INHERITED into it —
// so an implementor that satisfies an interface by inheriting the method from an
// unrelated ancestor (class Impl extends Base implements Handler, where Base, not a
// Handler subtype, declares handle) is still reached. The final java_method join
// only confirms the override's own name and arity (not that it is declared in sub).
// Covers interface/abstract dispatch, concrete-class overrides, and inherited-into-
// implementor satisfaction.
virtual_override(base, override) :- java_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, baseType, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, pc, _, _, _, base),
    name != "",
    baseType != "",
    type_ancestor(sub, baseType),
    sub != baseType,
    method_name_in_type(name, sub, override),
    override != base,
    java_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, pc, _, _, _, override).

// ── LIBRARY-declared base, CLIENT override (the callback / SAM shape) ────────
// The clause above reads java_method for the base, so it only fans CLIENT-declared bases.
// Every client implementation of a LIBRARY interface/superclass method — Runnable.run,
// Comparator.compare, Callable.call, Thread.run, ServletFilter.doFilter, a Spring callback —
// therefore had NO fan at all: a call on the library-typed receiver stopped at the empty
// library declaration and the client body was unreachable (measured: 06-anonymous-classes,
// task.run() / t.run(), two edges the JVM really executes).
// Pinned for cost: client_overridden_lib_type binds baseType to the FEW library types the
// client actually extends/implements, and the override must be a client INSTANCE_METHOD
// DECLARED in that subtype (no inherited-into fan, no lib->lib fan). java.lang.Object is
// excluded — fanning toString/equals/hashCode over every client override is the classic CHA
// blow-up and carries no dispatch information.
// lib_type (14): 2 qualifiedName 14 hash ; lib_method (22): 0 name 8 owner 18 paramCount 22 hash
client_overridden_lib_type(libType) :- java_type(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, sub),
    type_ancestor(sub, libType),
    lib_type(_, qname, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, libType),
    qname != "java.lang.Object".
// ABLATION-A (measuring): fully disabled to isolate this clause's cost at scale.
// virtual_override(base, override) :- client_overridden_lib_type(baseType),
//     lib_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, baseType, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, pc, _, _, _, base),
//     name != "",
//     type_ancestor(sub, baseType),
//     java_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, sub, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, "INSTANCE_METHOD", pc, _, _, _, override).

// Enum-constant fan-out (same enum type, no subtype edge — see method_override
// Clause 3): the enum's abstract method dispatches to each constant body.
virtual_override(base, override) :- java_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, enum, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, "ABSTRACT_METHOD", pc, _, _, _, base),
    name != "",
    java_method(name, _, _, _, _, _, _, enum, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, "ENUM_CONSTANT_METHOD", pc, _, _, _, override).
